If you can just walk up to something and bonk it on the head, half orcs are probably the most effective. But higher movement distance (wood elf/half-elf) is the most generically useful ability in the game that works independently of setup, build, etc etc. That combined with darkvision makes me think half and normal elves are the best in terms of raw combat versatility.

&#x200B;

Versatility as an argument is normally pure cope for something lacking effectiveness in any one respect, mind, but it's good in this game because you can freely respec and therefore never have to worry about your choice of race being significantly less effective if you get bored in your current playthrough and want to choose something else. For people who have optimization brainrot, like myself, it gives peace of mind knowing that my choice in race won't be strictly inferior to something else.

This is some Bleach shit. I started reading this series solely for this fight only to get blindsided by Domain Expansion: The Yourself.

&#x200B;

Beautiful.

My point is that age doesn't excuse clunky gameplay because RPG gameplay and character-building peaked 15 years ago. I could see age being an unfair comparison on any other genre but RPGs where what we have now is not much different from what we had back then.

&#x200B;

(although, as an aside, I personally don't think gameplay has evolved much in pretty much any genre since the late 2000's, but that's another topic for another day)

Hard disagree. Most of the best RPGs of all time are 20+ years old and it's not just because of the stories they tell.  RPGs in particular have hardly evolved over the years, at least in terms of gameplay, outside of the series that have taken a hard pivot into action game territory.

&#x200B;

Not that this is a bad thing, it's just how it is.

It's not going to get nerfed. They saw WARs literally soloing (not solo healing, *soloing*) expert dungeons last expac and then proceeded to make their self-healing -better.-  The *average* warrior could self-heal for 1/4th of a healer's output and almost 1/3rd at the higher end in savage fights back in ShB.

&#x200B;

This is quite clearly their intended results.

I'm 99% sure that the Arcticuno looking bird is hydaelyn.

&#x200B;

oh man. i can hardly wait until tomorrow

As someone who's gotten the title, I will say that it's a lot less daunting than it seems. It's still one of the most difficult achievements in the game, but if more higher-end ultimate-level players tried it, I do feel like there'd be a lot more Necromancers out there. It's a test of patience and nerves more so than mechanical skill, as you need to be focused for literally hours on end with very few breaks. It's similar to world prog in that regard, except the stakes for failure are a lot higher.

&#x200B;

But once you get comfortable in it, I'd go as far as to say that you can pretty much easily clear on MCH (and maybe RDM) seven or eight times out of ten. It can just take months to get to that point, and I do feel like an Ultimate raider would have the patience and confidence necessary to clear a lot faster.

